  Public API:

  [34mconst[39m prefs = [33mcreatePreferences[39m();   [32m// selects the best available adapter[39m
  prefs.[33msetUser[39m(username);             [32m// null or omitted = anonymous namespace[39m
  [34mawait[39m prefs.[33mget[39m(name, fallback);     [32m// fallback returned when unset[39m
  [34mawait[39m prefs.[33mset[39m(name, value);
  [34mawait[39m prefs.[33mremove[39m(name);
  [34mawait[39m prefs.[33mall[39m();                   [32m// the whole namespace as a plain object[39m
  prefs.[33misPersistent[39m();                [32m// false once degraded to memory[39m

  [38;5;153misPersistent()[39m stays synchronous on purpose — it's a property of the environment, not a stored value, and making a UI
  [38;5;153mawait[39m it to decide whether to show a warning would be noise.

  Storage key is [38;5;153mprefs:anon[39m or [38;5;153mprefs:user:<encodeURIComponent(username)>[39m — the encoding matters because usernames can
  contain colons and would otherwise collide across namespaces. The document is [38;5;153m{ v: 1, values: { … } }[39m; the version
  lives in the document rather than the key so a future migration can read old data and upgrade it in place instead of
  abandoning it. Values are whatever survives [38;5;153mJSON.stringify[39m — [38;5;153mset()[39m rejects [38;5;153mundefined[39m and functions rather than
  silently writing [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  Two things I want to put in front of you rather than bury:

  [1mAnonymous and logged-in preferences stay separate.[0m I'm not proposing an automatic merge on login — auto-merging means
  deciding whose value wins on conflict, and there's no evidence yet for either answer. If you later want it, [38;5;153mall()[39m plus
  [38;5;153mset()[39m makes it a handful of lines in the caller, with the merge policy visible where someone can argue with it.

  [1mA consequence of per-user namespacing worth naming now:[0m this app has no session persistence — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that
  returns an object and nothing survives a reload. So after a refresh the user is anonymous again and sees the anonymous
  namespace until they log in again, at which point their own preferences reappear. That's coherent, but it means
  "persists across sessions" holds per [3mlogged-in[0m session, not across reloads, until a real session or remember-me
  exists. I'd record that as a known limitation rather than try to fix it here, since fixing it properly means auth work
  well outside this request.

  Does that structure and API look right?

